    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "The goblin reference was just a little nod to the mischievous nature of fat bloom, but I suppose I should have been more precise. You see, in the world of chocolate making, goblins are often associated with the unpredictable and the troublesome, much like fat bloom.\n\nNow, about those tempering temperatures for dark chocolate... The ideal range for tempering dark chocolate is between 86°F (30°C) and 90°F (32°C) for the melting point, and 82°F (28°C) and 86°F (30°C) for the cooling point. However, the specific temperature range may vary depending on the type of dark chocolate you're working with, its cocoa butter content, and the desired final texture.\n\nFor example, a high-end dark couverture like yours might require a slightly narrower temperature range, around 85°F (29°C) to 88°F (31°C) for the melting point, and 80°F (27°C) to 84°F (29°C) for the cooling point. It's essential to consult your machine's user manual or a reliable chocolate making resource for specific guidance on tempering your particular chocolate.\n\nBy the way, have you considered the role of humidity in tempering",
